WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.888
COALESCE evaluates expressions from left to right
and returns the first one

00:00:03.912 --> 00:00:08.962
that is not NULL That makes it useful for fallback
logic A

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:12.875
preferred contact rule might try email first then
mobile phone then home

00:00:12.888 --> 00:00:18.912
phone and finally a readable message But COALESCE
only understands NULL An

00:00:18.938 --> 00:00:22.350
empty string or whitespace-only string is still
non-NULL and can stop the

00:00:22.375 --> 00:00:26.875
fallback too early That is why COALESCE is often
combined with NULLIF

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:32.425
and trimming Be careful with numeric defaults as
well Replacing a missing

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:36.925
bill with zero makes a strong domain assumption Use
a fallback only

00:00:36.938 --> 00:00:39.275
when its meaning is intentional and documented
